// case: generic-fn-as-value
// what: a generic has no one signature until a call fixes its parameters, so
//      a bare reference has nothing to infer from. Binding it would
//      monomorphise nothing and the value would carry an empty signature.
//      The binding is then called, because a program that binds the refusal
//      and never touches it pins only the declaration: the call is where the
//      widened site would print `not a function` about a name the refusal
//      never gave a callable type. The arm answers the poisoned spelling and
//      the call answers it again, so the refusal is the one diagnostic.
// expect-error: Cannot use generic function 'id' as a value
// one-diagnostic:
fn id[T](x: T) -> T {
    return x
}

fn main() -> int {
    val f = id
    return f(1)
}
